Z||000013||Monkeyman||12-22-1999||06:44 PM||||I'm not trying to pi** anyone off, but there aren't many of those around so the likelyhood that your kid or mine will be driving one is slim.  Until your kid's out on his own, don't let him have one and after that, he can make his own decisions.  I would think that having a lower roofline would make our already stable cars even more stable.  I have a 9 year old son and I wouldn't think of letting him have anything unsafe, but once he's out on his own, I can't control what he does although I can make suggestions.||||reg||1
Z||000014||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||07:19 PM||||Eddy,<P>Relax...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">... think happy thoughts :p we are all friends and adults here.  Firstly the way a roof looks does not necessarily imply its structural rigidity.  The perfect example of this is that fact that most people make the assumption based on looks that our beloved little cars are not crashworthy when in fact the opposite is true.  Our cars are more crashworthy than many larger vehicles.  Now for the stregnth of V8 Archies Chop top, I am confused about your point... Are you stating that since there is so little clearance between a passengers head and the roof that the car is dangerous?  That is somewhat true.  However being almost exactly 6' tall, I can tell you that my head firmly touches the roof of my fathers 1996 Ford Taurus (even while bent forward slightly).  This being the case, are you saying then that the new Ford Taurus is not safe?  Otherwise you are claiming that removing approximately 3" of vertical distance from the roof and laying back the windshild somehow decreases the structural integrity of the vehicle.  If this is your argument, I must admit that I am even more confused than I was before.  Such a slight change in angles does NOT sufficiently change the geometry to change the rigidity that our cars had coming from the factory.  And I end with a similar statement that I had before.  If the new welds (by Archie) are of good quality, then the chop top Fiero's posses the exact same structural rigidy as all of our Fiero's.<P>Furthermore, since the chop top kit is around $3k (if my memory serves me) not many people will be rushing to his shop to purchase one.... So you can sleep at night, your children most likely will not be driving one anytime soon.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000015||Monkeyman||12-22-1999||10:38 PM||||You know, 3 large for the chop top isn't really a bad price if it's good quality.  Not that I have 3 grand or would spend it on that even if I did, but if someone wants a truly unique Fiero, that would be the place to spend the money.||||reg||1
Z||000016||william_thorniii||12-23-1999||08:43 AM||||Monkey,<P>I would tend to agree with you, but he only lays back the windshield.  I think that for 3G's they should require a good core deposit, but have someone cut the glass.  then they should do the same with your other windows too.  If I remember correctly, your windows get replaced with Lexan (He might have changed this).  Although Lexan is the obvious alternative in the short term, it has some long term issues.  Yes, I would like to make a chop-top fastback.  I have thought about this for years.  Unfortunately, me being the perfectionist that I am would never settle for Archies shortcuts.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
